@style(../styles/IDocDemo.style)
@title(Code 8: randomized code)

`@@codegen` is a program template: setup lines draw values from the student's seed, the program is run on the server, and the key is what it really printed. `@@gencode` places it on the paper.

@box(w=100%, style=offline)
Each paper's answers come from running its program on the server.
@end

@variantlabel

@section(Values from the seed)

Setup lines start with the language's comment and `@@`; `@@(name)` puts a value into the code and `# @@ans` marks an output line as a question.

@codegen(python, id=loop)
#@ n = rand(3, 6)
#@ step = rand(2, 5)
total = 0
for i in range(@(n)):
    total += i * @(step)
print(total) # @ans
@end

@qc(10) What does this program print?
@gencode(loop)

@section(Blanks, words and checks)

`@@blank(code)` is a fill-in checked by running the student's line; `check` rejects a draw; `word("noun")` draws a word.

@codegen(python, id=words)
#@ w = word("noun")
#@ k = rand(2, 4)
#@ check k < 5
w = @(w:str)
print(w[:@(k)].upper()) # @ans
x = @blank(len(w))
print(x) # @ans
@end

@qc(10) Fill in the blank so that it prints the length of the word, and give the output.
@gencode(words)

@section(Formats and bit widths)

`hex16`, `dec8` and the other formats write a value in the language's own spelling; `splitbits` picks a field that always fits.

@codegen(c, id=field)
//@ v = rand(0, 65535)
//@ s, e = splitbits(16, 1, 3, 6)
#include <stdio.h>
int main(void) {
    unsigned v = @(v:hex16);
    unsigned f = (v >> @(s)) & ((1u << (@(e) - @(s) + 1)) - 1);
    printf("%X\n", f); // @ans
    return 0;
}
@end

@qc(10) What does this program print?
@gencode(field)

A second number places that many variants side by side, for a teacher's answer key.

@gencode(field, 2)

@section(A template in its own file)

`@@codepre(src=...)` reads a `.pre` file; the suffix before `.pre` names the language.

@qc(10) What does this module print?
@codepre(src=08-randomized-code.assets/xor8.sv.pre)
